description:
Found by #291's family-2 deletion.  The `__cond__` rename was not merely dodging a defvar clash — for a C-for CONDITION it was the ONLY thing that scoped the declaration.

REPRODUCER (perl "undef", PCL "DEFINED:3"):
  my $j = 3;
  for (my $i = 0; (my $k = $i) < $j; ++$i) { }
  print defined($k) ? "DEFINED:$k\n" : "undef\n";

EMISSION (with the rename deleted):
  (let (($i 0)) (p-for () ((p-< (p-scalar-= $k $i) $j)) ((p-incf-raw $i))))
  ... (p-defined $k)          ; reads the package cell the condition just wrote

The C-for INIT `my $i` gets its `let`; the CONDITION (and presumably the INCREMENT) `my` does not — it lowers to a bare assignment.  A `while (my $x = …)` DOES get one (probed: `(let (($name …)) (p-while (p-my-= $name …) …))`), so the gap is specific to the C-for head.

FIX: give the C-for condition/increment `my` a `let` around the whole `p-for`, the way the init `my` already gets one (perl re-declares it per iteration; a single let plus the per-iteration assignment matches for every shape the suite exercises — probe a closure-in-condition case to be sure).  Watch the VarAnnotator raw-slot verdict: the init counter is often raw, the condition-my must stay boxed unless proven.
